export const FIGURE_ITEMS_U6 = [
  {"id":"U6-D07","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"label","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-beamtrawl","q":"Label the beam, the net, the towing line and the seabed habitat that this gear disturbs.","extra":["floats holding the net up","drawstring closing the net"],"why":"The rigid beam holds the mouth of the net open, and the towing line joins the gear to the boat. The net is dragged along behind the beam, over the seabed, so the seabed habitat beneath it is crushed and disturbed. There are no floats or drawstring: those belong to a purse seine, which is set round a shoal near the surface."},
  {"id":"U6-D08","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-beamtrawl","target":"t-net","q":"Tap the part of this gear that is dragged along the seabed and catches the animals in its path.","why":"The net is dragged over the seabed behind the beam, so it scoops up almost everything it meets. The beam only holds the mouth of the net open, and the towing line joins the gear to the boat. A beam trawl is bottom (benthic) gear: it destroys seabed habitat and takes many non-target species."},
  {"id":"U6-D09","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-beamtrawl","q":"The drawing shows a beam trawl. Why does this method produce such a high level of bycatch?","options":["The net is dragged along the seabed and catches every organism in its path, target or not.","The net is towed at the surface, so it catches seabirds, turtles and dolphins that come up to breathe.","The boat travels very fast, colliding with animals that cannot get out of its way.","The heavy beam leaks oil into the water, poisoning organisms over a wide area."],"a":0,"why":"A beam trawl is non-selective bottom gear: dragged over the seabed, it takes everything in its path, so many non-target animals are caught. The drawing shows the net on the seabed, not at the surface; animals are caught by the net, not struck by the boat; and the beam is a metal bar, not a source of oil."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-01","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-purse","q":"A fishing crew catches tuna with the gear shown. Name the method.","options":["purse seine","drift net","pelagic trawl","cast net"],"a":0,"why":"A purse seine: a wall of net hangs from a ring of floats around the whole shoal, and a line along its bottom runs back to the boat so the net can be closed underneath. A drift net is a straight wall of net that drifts with the current; a pelagic trawl is a cone-shaped net towed behind a moving boat; a cast net is a small round net thrown by one person."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-02","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-purse","target":"p-draw","q":"Once the shoal is surrounded, the crew hauls in one part of this gear so the fish cannot escape downwards. Tap that part.","why":"The drawstring (purse line) runs through rings along the bottom of the net. Hauling it in closes the bottom like a purse, trapping the whole shoal. The floats only hold the top edge at the surface, the boat only sets the net in a ring round the shoal, and the net wall is what the drawstring closes, not the part hauled in."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-03","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-longline","q":"This gear is set from a boat and left in the water for several hours. Name the method.","options":["longline","trolling","pole and line","drift net"],"a":0,"why":"A longline: one long main line, held up by floats, carries very many short lines, each ending in a baited hook. Trolling pulls only a few lines with lures behind a moving boat; in pole-and-line fishing each fisher on the boat lands fish one at a time on a rod; a drift net is a wall of mesh, not hooks."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-04","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-longline","target":"l-hooks","q":"Seabirds, turtles and sharks are often caught by mistake with this method. Tap the part of the gear that catches them.","why":"The baited hooks attract any animal that takes the bait, so seabirds (as the line is set), turtles and sharks are hooked as bycatch. The main line and the floats only hold the hooks in place in the water."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-05","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-gillnet","q":"Name the fishing method shown in the drawing.","options":["gill net","purse seine","beam trawl","basket trap"],"a":0,"why":"A gill net: a wall of net stands on the seabed, held up by floats along its top and held down by weights and anchors, with buoys marking its ends. Fish swim into the mesh and are caught by their gills. A purse seine is closed around a shoal near the surface, a beam trawl is towed along the seabed by a moving boat, and a basket trap is a single baited container."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-06","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-gillnet","target":"g-anchors","q":"Tap the part of the drawing that shows this net is fixed in one place, rather than drifting with the current.","why":"The anchors hold the bottom of the net to the seabed, so it stays in one place: that makes it a gill net rather than a drift net, which hangs from floats and drifts freely. The floats keep the net upright and the buoys only mark where it is; neither stops it moving."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-07","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-trawls","q":"Both drawings show trawling. Which statement correctly names the two methods?","options":["Left: a beam trawl on the seabed. Right: a pelagic trawl in mid-water.","Left: a pelagic trawl on the seabed. Right: a beam trawl in mid-water.","Left: a beam trawl in mid-water. Right: a pelagic trawl on the seabed.","Left: a purse seine on the seabed. Right: a pelagic trawl in mid-water."],"a":0,"why":"On the left a rigid beam holds the net open as it is dragged along the seabed: a beam trawl. On the right a cone-shaped net is towed through mid-water, well clear of the seabed: a pelagic trawl. Swapping the names is the common slip; a purse seine is set in a ring around a shoal, not towed."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-08","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-pole-longline","q":"Both methods in the drawings can be used to catch tuna. Which method is more sustainable, and why?","options":["Left: fish are landed one at a time, so bycatch is low and catches stay small.","Right: its hooks are spread out, so it takes fewer fish from each part of the sea.","Left: its rods are made of wood, so the gear breaks down if it is lost at sea.","Right: its bait is chosen to suit tuna, so other species ignore the hooks."],"a":0,"why":"Pole and line (left) lands one fish at a time, so very little is caught by mistake and the catch is limited by how fast the crew can work. The longline (right) sets very many baited hooks, so it can take huge catches and also hooks seabirds, turtles and sharks. What the rods are made of does not make a method sustainable: its selectivity does."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-09","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-purse-pole","q":"Dolphins often swim with shoals of tuna. Which method is more likely to kill dolphins, and why?","options":["Left: the net surrounds the whole shoal and is closed, trapping dolphins too.","Right: the live bait thrown into the sea draws dolphins onto the hooks.","Left: dolphins mistake the floats at the surface for food and swallow them.","Right: the boat stays still, so dolphins hit it as they come up to breathe."],"a":0,"why":"The purse seine (left) is set around the whole shoal and closed underneath, so dolphins and turtles swimming with the tuna are trapped with it. In pole-and-line fishing (right) fish are taken one at a time on rods, so anything caught by mistake can be released. Floats and a stationary boat are not what kills dolphins."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-10","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-beam-trap","target":"bt-trawl","q":"Both kinds of gear in the drawings touch the seabed. Tap the gear that damages the largest area of seabed habitat while it is being used.","why":"The beam trawl net is dragged along the seabed behind a moving boat, crushing and burying seabed animals along its whole path. The basket trap also sits on the seabed, but it stays in one small spot, and the buoy only marks where it is."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-11","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-beam-trap","q":"A fisheries manager wants to protect a seabed habitat but still allow some fishing there. Why is the gear on the right the better choice?","options":["It stays in one place and catches only animals that go in, so little else is harmed.","It is made of natural material, so it cannot harm the seabed wherever it is put.","It is pulled along slowly, so seabed animals have time to move out of its way.","It is used in deeper water, below the depth where most seabed animals live."],"a":0,"why":"A basket trap is baited and left in one spot, so only the animals that enter it are caught and unwanted ones can be released alive. Its impact is low only if not too many are set and none are put on reefs, so it can still cause harm in the wrong place. It is not towed at all, and depth is not the reason: traps are set on the seabed where these animals live."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-12","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"tap","tier":2,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-gear4","target":"q-drift","q":"A storm breaks one of these kinds of gear free and it is lost at sea. Tap the gear most likely to go on entangling turtles and dolphins in its mesh for years.","why":"A lost drift net (a tangle net) becomes a ghost net: its wall of nylon mesh does not break down quickly, so it keeps entangling fish, turtles and dolphins for years. A cast net is small and is pulled back in by hand, a basket trap is too small for turtles and dolphins to enter, and trolling uses lines with lures, not mesh."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-13","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO2","fig":"G-U6-gear4","target":"q-troll","q":"Tap the only method shown that catches fish on lines, rather than in a net or a trap.","why":"Trolling is a line method: a few lines with lures are pulled behind a moving boat, some from long poles held out from the side. The cast net and the drift net are nets, and the basket trap is a trap."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-14","topic":"u6-fisheries","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"6.3/LO3","fig":"G-U6-sonar-radar","q":"A skipper wants to find a shoal below the boat and to spot other ships in fog. Which statement matches the two aids shown?","options":["Left: sonar, sound pulses that bounce off a shoal below. Right: radar, radio waves that bounce off ships.","Left: radar, radio waves that bounce off a shoal below. Right: sonar, sound pulses that bounce off ships.","Left: sonar, sound pulses that bounce off ships nearby. Right: radar, radio waves that bounce off fish below.","Left: GPS, satellite signals that find a shoal below. Right: radar, radio waves that give the depth of water."],"a":0,"why":"Sonar (left) sends sound pulses down through the water; they reflect from the seabed and from shoals, giving depth and the position of fish. Radar (right) sends radio waves through the air; they reflect from ships and coasts, so it works in fog. Swapping the two is the usual slip, and GPS gives the boat's own position, not where the fish are."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-15","topic":"u6-aquaculture","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"6.4/LO4","fig":"G-U6-openclosed","q":"Which picture shows an open aquaculture system, and why is it called open?","options":["Left: the net cage is in the sea, so water flows freely between farm and sea.","Right: the tanks are open at the top, so air and light can reach the fish.","Left: the cage is in an open bay that is not enclosed by the land around it.","Left: the net lets the farmed fish swim out to feed and then come back in."],"a":0,"why":"An open system keeps fish in cages or nets in natural water, so currents, waste, parasites and wild animals pass between the farm and the sea. The tanks on the right form a closed system: they are not connected to the sea, even if their tops are uncovered. Whether a bay is enclosed does not decide it, and farmed fish cannot leave the net."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-16","topic":"u6-aquaculture","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.4/LO2","fig":"G-U6-openclosed","q":"Parasites and disease can spread from farmed fish to wild fish. In which system is this more likely, and why?","options":["Left: sea water and wild fish pass close to the net, so parasites move both ways.","Right: the water is reused, so parasites build up and are pumped out to sea.","Left: the farmed fish leave the cage each night to feed, then swim back in.","Right: the filter traps parasites, which are then washed into nearby rivers."],"a":0,"why":"In the open net cage (left) the same sea water flows past farmed and wild fish, and wild fish gather around the cage, so parasites and disease pass between them. The closed tanks (right) are isolated from the sea: water is filtered and pumped round the tanks, not released. Farmed fish do not leave the cage, apart from accidental escapes."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-17","topic":"u6-aquaculture","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.4/LO2","fig":"G-U6-aquafarm","target":"a-net","q":"Uneaten food and fish waste from this farm can lower the oxygen in the water around it. Tap the part they pass through on their way into the sea.","why":"The net cage is open mesh, so uneaten food and faeces fall straight through it into the surrounding water and onto the seabed below, where their decomposition uses up oxygen. The cover net, floating ring, mooring lines and anchor blocks hold the farm in place or keep predators out; none of them carries waste."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-18","topic":"u6-aquaculture","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.4/LO5","fig":"G-U6-aquafarm","target":"a-cover","q":"Seabirds dive to take fish from cages like these. Tap the part of the farm that stops them.","why":"The cover net stretched over the top of each cage keeps diving seabirds away from the fish. The floating ring keeps the cage afloat, the net cage holds the fish in, and the mooring lines and anchor blocks hold the farm in place against currents and waves."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-19","topic":"u6-aquaculture","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.4/LO6","fig":"R-U6-02","q":"The diver is fixing small pieces of coral, grown in a nursery, onto a frame on a damaged reef. Why does this count as aquaculture?","options":["The corals are raised in controlled, protected conditions before being planted out.","The corals are grown so that they can be sold to aquariums for a profit later.","The corals are fed with fish pellets until they are big enough to move.","The corals are taken from a healthy reef and moved straight to a damaged one."],"a":0,"why":"Aquaculture means rearing organisms in controlled, protected conditions: the coral fragments are grown on in a nursery, safe from damage, and then planted out to restock the reef. Selling the coral is not what makes it aquaculture, corals are not fed pellets (their zooxanthellae make food by photosynthesis), and moving coral without growing it on is simple transplanting."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-20","topic":"u6-eutrophication","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.7/LO5","fig":"G-U6-seacage","q":"An algal bloom drifts over a fish farm like this one. When the bloom dies, the caged fish die before the wild fish nearby. Why?","options":["Decomposers use up the oxygen, and the caged fish cannot swim away to better water.","The bloom blocks the light, so the caged fish cannot see their food pellets.","The algae use up the nutrients, so the caged fish starve but wild fish do not.","The bloom warms the water, and only caged fish are sensitive to the heat."],"a":0,"why":"When the bloom dies, decomposers break down the dead algae and their aerobic respiration removes dissolved oxygen from the water. Wild fish can swim away to water with more oxygen, but fish held in a net cage cannot. Fish do not starve because algae take the nutrients, and a bloom does not kill by heating the water."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-21","topic":"u6-energy","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"6.5/LO2","fig":"G-U6-fossil-renew","q":"Which picture shows a non-renewable energy resource being obtained, and why is it non-renewable?","options":["Left: oil formed over millions of years, so it cannot be replaced once it is used.","Right: the turbines wear out, so they cannot be used again once they break.","Left: oil can be burned only once, so each barrel is used up as it is burned.","Right: wind and tides change every day, so this energy supply can run out."],"a":0,"why":"The left picture shows oil being drilled from rock beneath the seabed. Oil is a fossil fuel formed over millions of years, so it cannot be replaced once used up: that is what non-renewable means, not simply that a fuel is used once. Wind, waves and tides (right) will not run out, even though they vary, and worn-out turbines can be replaced."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-22","topic":"u6-energy","type":"multi","tier":2,"ref":"6.5/LO8","fig":"G-U6-fossil-renew","q":"Tick every disadvantage of obtaining energy with the devices in the right picture.","options":["Underwater cables are needed to carry the electricity to land.","They give out carbon dioxide while they generate electricity.","Fixing the devices to the seabed and laying cables can damage it.","They can be built at any point along any coast.","Marine animals can be injured by the moving turbine blades.","Once installed, they need no maintenance or repairs."],"a":[0,2,4],"why":"Ocean renewables need underwater cables to carry the electricity ashore, their bases and cables damage the seabed, and moving turbines can injure marine life. They do not give out carbon dioxide as they generate (that is an advantage), they can be built only where wind, waves or tidal currents are strong enough, and storms and salt water mean they need costly maintenance."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-23","topic":"u6-plastic","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.6/LO3","fig":"R-U6-01","target":"y-centre","q":"Plastic washed into the sea off East Asia floats at the surface and is carried by the currents of the North Pacific gyre. Tap where it will build up into a garbage patch.","why":"The circulating currents of the gyre carry floating plastic round and towards the calm centre of the loop, where it collects into a garbage patch. It does not stay near the coast where it entered, it keeps moving while it is in the current itself, and the equator is outside this gyre."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-24","topic":"u6-plastic","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.6/LO3","fig":"R-U6-01","q":"Each loop on the map is a gyre. Why does plastic litter build up near the centre of a gyre?","options":["The circling surface currents move floating plastic inwards to the calm centre.","The water is deepest at the centre, so the plastic sinks there and piles up.","Tidal currents carry plastic off the coasts twice a day and drop it there.","Currents along the seabed drag plastic across the ocean floor to the centre."],"a":0,"why":"A gyre is a huge loop of circulating surface currents. Floating plastic is carried round and drawn towards the calm centre, where the water barely moves, so it collects there as a garbage patch. Gyres are surface currents, not tidal or seabed currents, and the plastic in a patch is floating, not sinking."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-25","topic":"u6-climate","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"6.8/LO5","fig":"R-U6-03","target":"m-iceB","q":"Tap the ice that models an ice sheet melting on land.","why":"The ice on the mesh above beaker B is not in the water, like an ice sheet on land: as it melts, its water runs into the beaker and adds to it. The ice floating in beaker A models sea ice, which is already in the water."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-26","topic":"u6-climate","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.8/LO5","fig":"R-U6-03","q":"Beaker B models one cause of sea-level rise. Which other cause of sea-level rise is not modelled by either beaker?","options":["Sea water expanding as it is warmed","Floating sea ice melting into the sea","Faster evaporation from a warmer sea","Melting ice cooling the sea so it shrinks"],"a":0,"why":"As sea water absorbs heat it expands (thermal expansion): the same mass takes up more volume, so sea level rises. Melting floating sea ice, as in beaker A, does not raise the level, because the ice already displaces its own mass of water. Evaporation would lower the level, not raise it, and a sea that contracted would fall."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-27","topic":"u6-climate","type":"tap","tier":2,"ref":"6.8/LO2","fig":"G-U6-greenhouse","target":"h-sun","q":"Tap the arrow showing energy that passes straight through the layer of greenhouse gases on its way down to the Earth's surface.","why":"Sunlight passes through the atmosphere, including the greenhouse gases, and warms the surface. Greenhouse gases do not block incoming sunlight; they absorb the heat radiated up from the warmed surface and send some of it back down, which is the arrow from the gas layer to the surface."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-28","topic":"u6-climate","type":"tap","tier":2,"ref":"6.8/LO4","fig":"G-U6-greenhouse","target":"h-ocean","q":"Tap the arrow showing heat taken down into the ocean water and stored there, which makes the water expand and raises sea level.","why":"The arrow into the sea shows heat absorbed by the ocean. Warmed water expands, so the same mass of water takes up more volume and sea level rises. The ocean taking up this heat also keeps global temperature more stable. The sunlight and returning-heat arrows only reach the surface, and the other two show heat leaving the surface or escaping to space."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-29","topic":"u6-climate","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.8/LO2","fig":"G-U6-greenhouse","q":"More carbon dioxide is added to the layer of greenhouse gases. Why does the Earth then warm up?","options":["More heat from the surface is absorbed and sent back down, so less escapes.","The thicker layer blocks more sunlight, so more heat stays near the ground.","Carbon dioxide reacts with oxygen in the air, and the reaction gives out heat.","The layer reflects more sunlight down onto the sea, which absorbs it twice."],"a":0,"why":"Extra greenhouse gas absorbs more of the heat radiated up from the surface and radiates more of it back down, so less escapes to space: the enhanced greenhouse effect. If the gases blocked or reflected sunlight they would cool the surface, and carbon dioxide does not react with oxygen to make heat."},
  {"id":"PQ-U6-30","topic":"u6-conservation","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"6.9/LO3","fig":"R-U6-02","q":"The diver is fixing farmed coral to a frame to restore a damaged reef. Which is a limitation of this project?","options":["The new coral may also die if the cause of the damage, such as warming, continues.","Farmed coral lacks zooxanthellae, so it may starve once it is on the reef.","Farmed coral grows so fast that it may crowd out and kill the wild coral nearby.","The frames attract predators, so the number of reef fish usually falls afterwards."],"a":0,"why":"Coral farming only works if the cause of the damage has stopped: if the water stays too warm or polluted, the new coral dies too. It is also slow, costly and needs skilled divers. Farmed coral does contain zooxanthellae, it does not out-compete wild coral, and restored reefs usually gain fish rather than lose them."},

];
